A||N||11||kornholio||matching an amp to subwoofers||||5||
Z||000000||kornholio||09-20-2000||06:21 PM||||Here's a question for the car audio experts...<P>I don't know much about how to match an amplifier to the subs.  I know that if you wire two 4 ohm subs in parallel that you will end up with 2 ohms.  So does that mean I need an amp that specifies 2 ohm bridged?<P>And about the power output, if those two subs  have a power handling of 600 watts and the amp has a bridged output of 150 watts, does that mean that the amp is not powerful enough?<P>Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Seanh||09-20-2000||09:18 PM||||Ok, well, if you are going to hook up in parallel you should find an amp that is optimized for a 2 omh briged output. I am running 2 12's behind my Fiero seats and they are 8 omh and hook in par. to 2 omh load. I used Premier PRS amp on them, they are design for a 2 omh output. To answer your question, yes, you shoud have an amp optimized for 2 omh briged output. Your second question. If your subs are capable of 600 watts (is that each or together?) I would get an amp that puts out that kind of power. Less power to your speakers is worse then too much power because if there not getting the power they need, and you crank up the music, the voice coils will get thrown around in there and break, thus blowing your speaker. Keep in mind most amps are rated in 4 omh output, so an amp capable of 150 watts at 4 omhs briged would be 300 watts at 2 omhs briged. What speakers do you have and I might be able to help you find a suitable amp. Email me at sean@kdsi.net||||reg||1
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||09-20-2000||09:26 PM||||Also, note as to weather that 600 watts is peak or RMS. Peak means they can take a 'hit' of 600 watts, but runn them like that for too long and you'll be left with 2 12 inch pieces of junk. I have a 4x500 amp (4 channels at 125 watts) 2 6x9 pioneers (4 ohm peek 250 rms 125) and 2 generic 12's (which I'm running at 4 ohm, 125 watts just because I'm not sure how much the can handle) <P>Seanh, what kind of 12's are you running? and how tall are you? I want to put my 12's in the fiero, but even with cutting the 'c' channel, it'll be close (I'm just short of 6')||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||09-20-2000||09:38 PM||||I am running Polk Momo 12's. My seat were only affected very little at most, but for the most part, I dont have any trouble with the seats, they still go back all the way. BTW, I had room to spare behind the subs. I am 5'10".||||reg||1
Z||000004||olympic||09-21-2000||02:04 AM||||  My experience has been that you need to give each sub at least 200 watts(rms) to get real hard hitting bass.  <P>  You'll need an amp rated  to be stable down to 2 ohms bridged or 1 ohm stereo. Some good choices for low impedance amps are phoenix gold, MMats, and the new alpines.  <P>   If your amp is rated at 150 watts bridged into 4 ohms it should put out 200-300 watts bridged into 2 ohms(if it will drive a 2 ohm load, that is).  Give this setup a try.  You may find it sufficient.  Just keep an eye on your amp.  If it cuts out or gets real hot you'll need a better amp.<p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 09-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||09-21-2000||03:33 AM||||I'm going to indulge myself...Beware: <B>Cynical veiwpoint ahead!</B> Not specifically intended to <I>piss anybody off</I> but ... do as you will.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>First up, Sean...so nice to see you again! Whatever you do, don't upset stimpy again. We just got him all calmed down and back on his feed!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Onward! I am an old live sound reinforcement guy from way back. Concerts, theatre, broadcast TV, post production and sound studios. Not trying to read you my resume, just creating a contrast. I'm not an engineer in those fields either so I am not claiming that <I>everything</I> I may state here to be factual truth but I do have a clue.<P>I remember one sleepy morning suffering from major jetlag, many years ago when I first came to the U.S., being woken up in a northern L.A. suburb apartment by this bloody great <B>thump, thump, thump, thump.</B> I thought it was an earthquake! I $hit you not...it was somebodys car stereo for crying out loud. I could not believe it. Now I know that it must have sounded like crap in that car. The rattling door panels and windows aside, the distortion alone would drive me nuts. I'm a purist to some degree...also a musician in the past. I luv loud!!! But I luv loud and clear! Not 'Louder than you' if you get my drift. That thumping piece of $hit was all about showing someone 2 blocks over how much noise we can make...not for the enhancement of the pleasure of listening to music. I think I read on the forum about someone entering a recent stereo install in a competition where on of the criteria was "overall loudness". Did that include clarity? I dont know but wonder??<P>To the point...Kornholio, I commend you on wanting to, hopefully, enhance your listening experience and want to help if possible...here's a couple of elements to keep in mind as you go.<P>WATTS...Not an acronym for <B>Way Asscool Thunder Thumpin'</B>etc...it is a measurement of energy. In this case, electrical energy.<P>VOLTS...or EMF ( Electro motive force )<P>RMS...Root Mean Square. This is an average measurement utilized in audio, amongst other things, due to constantly fluctuating levels.<P>PEAK...a half step before "<B>BANG</B> in this case anyways.<P>I assume you want to beef things up and when you crank up the stock system it starts sounding fuzzy and distorted. This is usually caused by 2 things. Old and tired speakers flapping around in their frames and transistor distortion. The latter is due to the lower frequencies draining the voltage from the amplifier in basic terms. To elaborate, bass frequency (20hz to say 200hz) is a long slow sound wave(s) that requires much more energy to move it in volume than its higher counterparts. This equates to more voltage or energy internal to the components of the amplifier to push the speaker cone hard enough to move the air to create the sound. This drain reduces overall power to the mosfet transistors for brief periods and causes distortion in the more audible frequency range. The fiX?? Remove the bass components from the rest of the dynamic range and give it its own amp, power feed etc.<P>Choosing components to do this effectivley is not all equated directly to big watts and big speakers etc. It is related more to quality components with better "sensitivity" performance. Translated this means simply lower input, higher output at lower distortion figures; or efficiency! Kinda like you can put a 454 Chevy big block in your Fiero just to be sure you have enough power but...is it necessary when there are smaller, more efficient engines to be had that will provide more than adequate power??<P>I'm looking at improving my sound in my GT soon and my approach will be something like this; A couple of subs probably 8" rated around a true 75 to 100 watts with a 4 ohm impedance. Somewhere to put 'em! Well, options are kinda limited in a Fiero so that leaves behind the seats as you are looking at and built into a solid ported enclosure with a cubic foot capacity suitable to the speaker manufacturors specs. Provide decent heavy gauge stranded wire to each from the amp and make the lengths for both sides the same. Purchase a quality dual power amp that will provide approx. 10% to 20% more power than the sub speakers are rated for based on 4 ohms. There are many amps that claim to be stable down to 2 ohms but they sure dont look stable on an oscilliscope. Rating the amp slightly over the speakers is to make the bass drivers "breathe". Just 'cause the knob goes up  to 10 does not mean its meant to be run there right?? Make sure polarity is correct on amp and speakers. If positive/negative are transposed anywhere it will work but sound like $hit! It's sorta like runnin' your Fiero in reverse all the way to work in the morning. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>With the bottom end taken care of, place decent quality replacement speakers in the OEM locations, 45 to 55 watt RMS 2 ways in the sail panels and 60 to 75 watt RMS 3 ways in the dash. Run an active x-over to the sub amp and a 60 to 70 watt RMS amp for the sail panel speakers. Use these as low-mid to high drivers only and again rate the amp around 10% over that of the speaker rating. Also 4 ohm speakers. (Dual amp = stereo or 2 in one) Say 60 watts RMS per side...for sail panels. Adequate for this frequency range.<P>For the front dash speakers, either utilize the amp in your existing CD or whatever if powerful enough, or a separate amp but theses will run the whole dynamic range. Use the same principals covered above. The last component for me would be a simple but clean Graphic for fine tuning. Make all 12v connections clean and run a decent gauge stranded wire that can deliver sufficient dc surge for bottom end punch. Fuse everything, yada, yada, yada.<P>I've read lots of sound stuff on this forum and the only one that sticks in my mind as having a clue was Fierosound. He mentioned key phrases like "sensitivity" and efficiency in context. He had a website totally dedicated to a major stereo upgrade in his Fiero including sub boxes behind the seats. Do a search for his name here on the Forum or maybe he will chip in here if he's still around...either way, although I thought his install was a bit overboard, he knew what he was doing and why! Full dolby surround if I remember correctly. More to the point, he had the quality component names to give you and where to get 'em, how much etc. I know what I want when I see it...I'm not inclined to rip off a zillion part numbers like our buddy Sean here...Frankly Sean, I believe you do some stereo installs but you are yet to convince me that you truly have a clue! Sorry bud...Still have your little Twin Turbo thing fresh in mind! <P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000006||kornholio||09-21-2000||07:16 AM||||Thanks for all the input.  I haven't bought my subs yet.  I was looking at the Kenwood 10" Tornado subs and wondering what amp would  be powerful enough to give a "thump" that you can feel.  Keep in mind that I am on a budget.  By the way, I wasn't planning on ripping my Fiero apart to keep a stock look.  I was just going to stick the sub box in my passenger foot area since I rarely have passengers.  I've been told that I would not get very good sound in that location, but I just want some bass.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000007||286SE'S||09-21-2000||03:50 PM||||Why doesn't any one mention the sensitivity component of the Subs in question to see if they are match with the amp? I see Subs with as little as 85db sensitivity and some as high as 95db. Every 3db of higher sensitivity is equal to doubling the power of your amp.<BR>So a 87db sub with a 300 watt amp should play as loud as a 93db sub with a 100 watt amp.<BR>A statement like "a sub needs at least 200 watts of power to sound good" is not going to work if the speakers are very low in sensitivity. <BR>Another thing I should mention is that there is NO LAW that says an automotive stereo manufacturer has to correctly state the power output of an amp (unlike home audio which is strictly enforced). They are getting better at being honest but I just recently got stuck  with a supposed 300 watt RMS amp that didn't even have a mosfet power supply (actual output 'maybe' 20 watts.) Stick with name brands!<BR>Terry<BR>AAAGH! had to add this;<BR>Someone said not enough power will "rattle the speakers apart" This is only true with tweeters. A distorted signal from an overdriven amp will destroy the voice coils of a tweeter which are more vulnerable due to their tiny components. <p>[This message has been edited by 286SE'S (edited 09-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||09-21-2000||05:47 PM||||286SE's: I dont want to piss you off. but, your right about the tweeters, but it will also occur with subs and mids. because the louder you crank up your stereo, the more distortion you will have, at a certain piont this distortion comming from either your amp or your headunit, most likly the Amp. will cause your voice coil to rattle and get thrown around inside the speaker. Thus it will break over time, not right then.||||reg||1
Z||000009||baldlobo||09-21-2000||05:50 PM||||has anyone put their amps under their seats?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000010||olympic||09-21-2000||09:36 PM||||oops....I've edited my first post to read "at least 200 watts rms"  I forgot a word there.  But regardless, I've had experience with several types of subs(sealed, ported, infinite baffle) and I've never found one that would really perform well with less than 200 watts.  Some people may only want to add some depth to their music so a single 8" sub powered by a 100 watt amp may be enough.  But I got the impression that kornholio wasn't looking for a little depth but real hard hitting bass.  I don't care if a sub has a sensitivity of 100 db 1w/1m it won't hit hard with only 50 or 100 watts.  And 286se's is right about the lower sensitivity part.  My current subs are rated at about 86 db 1w/1m and I am driving them with about 500 watts each which is barely enough to push the subs to their limit.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>So a 87db sub with a 300 watt amp should play as loud as a 93db sub with a 100 watt amp.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>   Not necessarily.  Different subs have different power compression curves.  They do not respone in a linear fashion to an increase in power input.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>A statement like "a sub needs at least 200 watts of power to sound good" is not going to work if the speakers are very low in sensitivity.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I didn't say "to sound good",  I said "to hit hard".  Big difference.  And I did say "at least 200 watts" because I know a low sensitivity sub may need 500 watts or more to realize its potential.<P>And I'll agree that the power ratings of car audio amps has gotten alot better in the past few years.  Most quality amps made by reputable companies are now under rated by quite a bit. My Alpine 1505 is rated at 600 watts bridged into 4 ohms @ 12v.  I had it tested and it put out 946 watts.  So with my cars voltage at around 14 volts it's easily capable of 1200 watts, doubling its rated output.  When buying an amp you have to look at the dollars/watt ratio.  If it's too low from the norm be suspicious.<P>And finally, a few pics of my cars show winning system.  100% built and installed by a guy who doesn't have a clue(me)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topaz04_2.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topaz05_1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topaz06_1.jpg"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ozzy||09-22-2000||02:03 AM||||I guess all I can add is to say the interpretation of sound quality is as subjective as music taste itself. This is illustrated to me thru your statement; <P>I didn't say "to sound good", I said "to hit hard". Big difference.<P>I kinda thought I liberated the sensitivity point too....<P>Does not matter. Enjoy!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
